    {
      "slug": "reddit-unpaid-driveway-lien",
      "script": "Who froze the closing? I paved a driveway for a general contractor on net-30. Then he went silent for two months while selling that exact property for eight hundred grand. Emails ignored. Calls ignored. He forgot that I still had the signed work order, and state law let unpaid subcontractors file a mechanic's lien against the property, something most GCs forget. So I filed it three days before closing. The title company found it in escrow and froze the whole sale. His buyer's lawyer started screaming, and an invoice ignored for two months got paid in full within four hours, plus statutory penalty interest. He called it a misunderstanding. I called it pending. The lien release left only after the wire cleared. It took nine minutes. Would you ignore the driveway invoice again?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a four-word question hook, tightened the lien escalation, and landed on the driveway invoice while preserving every amount and interval."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-pharmacy-coupon-not-an-employee",
      "script": "A badge means employee? A woman cornered me by the pharmacy counter with a crumpled coupon and demanded I ring her up because my badge clearly meant I worked there. I didn't. The lanyard was from a 5k that morning; I was picking up my own prescription. She kept talking over me, so I radioed my invisible manager and escorted her to the actual register. I told the real cashier she'd been waiting and handed her off like my shift depended on it. The cashier played along, then discovered the coupon had expired two years earlier. On her way out, I admitted I didn't work there; I just had a helpful face. She froze for ten seconds, spotted a real employee in the same color shirt, and chased him instead. Pharmacy lanyard: proof of employment?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a four-word question hook, added one dry beat, and returned to the pharmacy lanyard while preserving 5k, two years, and ten seconds."
    }
  ],
